// Anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. No APM — relying on uptime-only:
//    UptimeRobot says "200 OK", checkout is broken because the payment API blocks.
//    → Add New Relic or Tideways to staging AND production.

// 2. No timeouts on external API calls in checkout:
//    $response = wp_remote_get('https://payment.example.com/api');
//    → checkout hangs indefinitely when the remote is slow
//    → always add: ['timeout' => 5] to wp_remote_get() args

// 3. WP_Query without posts_per_page limit:
//    new WP_Query(['post_type' => 'product']); // no limit
//    → pulls every product into memory; server load explodes

entries.code_example:
# Nginx FastCGI page cache configuration (excerpt) for anonymous WordPress users.
# Place in the server {} block for the WordPress vhost.

fastcgi_cache_path /var/cache/nginx/wp levels=1:2 keys_zone=wp_cache:100m inactive=60m;
fastcgi_cache_key "$scheme$request_method$host$request_uri";

server {
    # ... standard WordPress Nginx config ...

    set $skip_cache 0;

    # Never cache logged-in users, cart, checkout, or admin.
    if ($request_uri ~* "/(cart|checkout|wp-admin|wp-login)") {
        set $skip_cache 1;
    }
    if ($http_cookie ~* "wordpress_logged_in|woocommerce_cart_hash") {
        set $skip_cache 1;
    }

    location ~ \.php$ {
        fastcgi_cache           wp_cache;
        fastcgi_cache_valid      200 1h;
        fastcgi_cache_bypass     $skip_cache;
        fastcgi_no_cache         $skip_cache;
        add_header X-FastCGI-Cache $upstream_cache_status;
        # ... fastcgi_pass, fastcgi_param etc. ...
    }
}

entries.bad_pattern:
// Caching anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. Caching the cart or checkout page for all users:
//    fastcgi_cache_valid 200 1h;  (no exclusions)
//    → User A sees User B's cart contents. Never cache stateful pages.

// 2. Purging the entire cache on every content update:
//    → A single post save triggers a full Redis flush.
//    → Purge only the affected pages and their related archive/taxonomy pages.

// 3. Edge cache (CDN) and page cache not aligned:
//    → CDN caches a page that the page cache has already invalidated.
//    → CDN now serves stale HTML for the CDN TTL.
//    → Define cache rules consistently in one place and propagate them.

// 4. Deploying without warming the cache:
//    → First real visitor triggers full PHP + MySQL execution for every page.
//    → Automate a sitemap-based warm-up pass immediately after every deploy.

// Heartbeat anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. Default Heartbeat (15 s) with 10 concurrent editors in the backend:
//    → 40+ requests/min to admin-ajax.php
//    → PHP workers saturate; frontend requests queue behind backend polls

// 2. Heartbeat enabled on the frontend with no WooCommerce dependency:
//    → Every anonymous page view triggers a Heartbeat poll on the next admin-ajax.php hit
//    → No benefit, only overhead

// 3. Disabling Heartbeat entirely on WooCommerce checkout:
//    wp_deregister_script('heartbeat'); // on all pages, no exceptions
//    → WooCommerce uses Heartbeat to refresh session and stock on checkout
//    → Silent stock / session mismatch bugs; disable only on non-WooCommerce frontend pages

// Security hardening anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. Username "admin" still active with a weak password:
//    → automated bots find it in hours. Rename or delete the "admin" account on every install.

// 2. Shared team logins:
//    → when a team member leaves, there is no offboarding — one password change breaks everyone
//    → every person gets an individual account

// 3. DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT not set:
//    → an attacker who gets into wp-admin can immediately edit theme files and inject PHP
//    → define('DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT', true) in wp-config.php on every project

// 4. SSH with password authentication on production:
//    PasswordAuthentication yes  # in /etc/ssh/sshd_config
//    → brute-force risk. Disable: PasswordAuthentication no; use key-only auth.

// 5. WPScan never runs in CI:
//    → a plugin with a known CVE stays live until a client is compromised

// Logging and alerting anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. WP_DEBUG_LOG in production but with WP_DEBUG_DISPLAY also true:
//    define('WP_DEBUG', true);
//    define('WP_DEBUG_DISPLAY', true);  // ← exposes stack traces to visitors
//    → always set WP_DEBUG_DISPLAY to false on non-development environments

// 2. All events in one log file, no separation:
//    → warnings, info, security events, and PHP fatal errors mixed together
//    → when you need the log in an incident, it's 2 GB of noise

// 3. Alerts via email only:
//    → "We haven't had orders in two weeks" — the client calls before the developer reads the email
//    → route critical events to Slack or Pushover so the whole team sees them in real time

// 4. No log rotation:
//    → wp-content/debug.log grows to several GB; disk full; site goes down
//    → rotate monthly via cron or a logrotate config

// Backup anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. Backup stored only on the live server:
//    tar -czf /var/www/backups/site.tar.gz /var/www/html
//    → if the server fails, disk corrupts, or is hacked, the backup is gone with it
//    → always sync to an offsite location (S3, B2, another provider)

// 2. Never testing the restore:
//    → the backup "exists" but nobody has ever actually imported it
//    → a restore test is not "the command ran without errors"
//    → it is: can you log in, navigate, and complete a checkout after the restore?

// 3. Backup on the same hosting region:
//    → a regional data center outage (power, fire, flood) takes both the server and the backup
//    → offsite storage must be in a different geographic region

// 4. No tiered restore plan:
//    → restoring the full site when only the DB is corrupt wastes hours
//    → document the three scenarios: files-only, DB-only, full restore

// Performance budget anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. No written budgets — "it feels fast":
//    → one year after launch, TTFB has drifted from 1.8 s LCP to 4.5 s
//    → nobody noticed because there was no number to check against

// 2. Budgets defined but not in CI:
//    → the definition exists in a Notion document nobody reads
//    → the build passes even when a new plugin doubles query count

// 3. Redis hit rate below 85% — never investigated:
//    → 62% of requests go straight to the database
//    → cause was missing locale in cache keys; easily fixed once visible

// 4. No pre-warm after deploy:
//    → TTFB spikes for 15–20 minutes after every deploy
//    → these are exactly the minutes when Googlebot crawls; bad Core Web Vitals signals

// WP-Cron anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. WP-Cron on a low-traffic site:
//    → A daily import job that runs "every midnight" never fires on a quiet Tuesday night
//    → DISABLE_WP_CRON is not set, system cron is not configured
//    → the import runs at noon on Wednesday when the next visitor arrives

// 2. WP-Cron on a high-traffic site:
//    → 100 requests per second all check _cron_event and all find it "due"
//    → the same import runs 100 times simultaneously; database deadlocks

// 3. Missing DISABLE_WP_CRON after setting up system cron:
//    define('DISABLE_WP_CRON', true); // missing
//    → WP-Cron still fires via visitor requests AND system cron fires separately
//    → duplicate execution; data integrity issues

// 4. No monitoring on the cron job:
//    → the system cron silently fails (disk full, PHP error)
//    → nobody notices until a client reports missing emails or stale data

// Automation anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. Admin user running cron jobs:
//    # crontab -e -u root
//    * * * * * wp --path=/var/www/html --allow-root cron event run --due-now
//    → running WP-CLI as root gives an exploit unlimited system access
//    → use a dedicated low-privilege service account

// 2. Playbook not in version control:
//    → the maintenance script lives on the server, not in the repo
//    → no code review, no rollback, a new team member has no idea it exists

// 3. No Slack alert on job failure:
//    → the backup job silently fails for two weeks (disk full)
//    → nobody notices until a disaster requires a restore

// 4. API keys hardcoded in the script:
//    SOMEAPI_KEY="live_abcdef1234" # in bin/maintenance.sh
//    → committed to git, visible in CI logs
//    → use environment variables (${SOMEAPI_KEY}) loaded from hosting secrets or .env

// Hosting and server configuration anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. PHP 7.4 or older on production in 2025+:
//    → PHP 8.2 is up to 20% faster than 7.4 on the same hardware
//    → older versions lose security support; unfixed CVEs

// 2. OPcache disabled or at default settings:
//    opcache.max_accelerated_files=10000 (default)
//    → a WooCommerce + 40 plugins install has more than 10 000 PHP files
//    → files that don't fit the cache are re-parsed on every request

// 3. innodb_buffer_pool_size at default (128 MB) on a server with 8 GB RAM:
//    → 128 MB fits almost nothing; MySQL hits disk constantly
//    → set to 4–5 GB (50–70% of RAM) for a database-heavy WordPress project

// 4. Redis key collisions in Multisite:
//    wp_cache_set('latest_news', $data, 'my-group');  // no blog_id, no locale
//    → Blog 1 (de_DE) and Blog 3 (en_US) share the same key; one overwrites the other
//    → key must include get_current_blog_id() and get_locale()

// Autoload bloat anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. Caching API responses in an autoloaded option:
//    update_option('weather_api_cache', $response_body); // autoload=yes (default)
//    → 2.6 MB loaded on every single request, including pages that never display weather

// 2. Making a live API call on admin_init for license checks:
//    add_action('admin_init', static function (): void {
//        $valid = wp_remote_get('https://license.example.com/check');
//    });
//    → every admin page load blocks on an external HTTP call
//    → if the license server is slow or down, the entire admin is slow or broken
//    → move to WP-Cron: once daily, cache result with autoload=no

// 3. Never auditing autoload size:
//    → after 2 years of plugin installs, autoload totals 4 MB
//    → nobody ran the SQL query to see what was there

// Daily health check anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. No daily check at all — "it's running fine":
//    → a shop went five days with no orders because a payment webhook failed silently
//    → "it's running fine" is the most dangerous sentence in web development

// 2. Checking only uptime — ignoring error logs and backup status:
//    → UptimeRobot says 100% uptime
//    → the backup job has been failing for three weeks
//    → when a disaster hits, there is no restorable backup

// 3. Investigating security alerts "later in the afternoon":
//    → unusual login attempts spotted at 9 AM, analyzed at 5 PM
//    → the attacker had 8 hours of undisturbed access

// 4. Alerts via email that get ignored:
//    → 50 monitoring emails per day → all go to a folder nobody reads
//    → route critical alerts to Slack with a named owner who must respond

// Crisis management anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. The client is your alarm system:
//    → "We haven't had orders in two weeks" — the first anyone on the team heard of it
//    → monitoring must detect and alert the team before the client notices

// 2. No defined incident commander:
//    → a compromised site triggers a group discussion about "what to do"
//    → three people fix things at the same time; some fixes conflict
//    → one person decides; everyone else executes the runbook

// 3. Reinstalling core without killing sessions first:
//    wp core download --force  // without: wp user session destroy --all
//    → if the attacker has an active session, they can undo your reinstall immediately

// 4. No post-mortem:
//    → the incident is resolved; everyone moves on
//    → the same vulnerability is exploited three months later
//    → every incident must produce a post-mortem: cause, runbook adjustment, monitoring improvement

// Agency operations anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. No module owner — bugs bounce around:
//    → A payment bug is reported. Developer A says "that's B's code."
//       B says "I haven't touched that in months." Five days pass.
//    → Assign an owner in README.md. Alerts go to them. They fix it.

// 2. Every project has its own CI pipeline variant:
//    → 12 projects, 12 slightly different GitHub Actions files.
//    → A security fix must be applied to each one by hand.
//    → Central template + one-time apply. Fixes propagate in minutes.

// 3. No release train — deploys happen whenever:
//    → A developer pushes to production on a Friday afternoon "because the ticket was done."
//    → No tests. No client notice. Support calls begin Monday morning.
//    → Fixed schedule: every two weeks, Tuesday 10 AM, after code freeze and tests.

// 4. Manual plugin updates across the fleet:
//    → An agency has WooCommerce on 15 sites. A security patch releases.
//    → Each site updated manually over three days. Three sites break.
//    → Private Composer registry + wave rollout: patch once, deploy cleanly everywhere.

// SEV matrix and runbook anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. No severity classification — all bugs are "urgent":
//    → The client reports a cosmetic sidebar issue as "urgent."
//    → The on-call developer drops a critical payment fix to work on sidebar CSS.
//    → SEV matrix: the matrix classifies both and the payment fix stays priority.

// 2. No runbooks — incident response starts with a group discussion:
//    → "Which log do we check?" "Who knows the cron config?"
//    → Every incident takes 30 minutes longer than it should.
//    → Runbooks answer these questions in advance. The team executes, not debates.

// 3. Monitoring relies on the client as the alarm system:
//    → "We haven't had orders in two days."
//    → The checkout was broken for 48 hours before anyone on the team knew.
//    → Monitoring must detect and alert the team before the client notices.

// 4. No post-mortem after resolution:
//    → "It's fixed, let's move on."
//    → Three months later: the same cron misconfiguration recurs on a different project.
//    → Every SEV 1 and SEV 2 incident ends with a post-mortem and runbook update.

// Content workflow anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. No roles beyond default Author/Editor:
//    → Any editor can publish directly to production without approval.
//    → A draft that wasn't ready went live during a product launch.
//    → Add a Publisher role with PublishPress; editors can approve, only publishers push live.

// 2. No visible workflow states:
//    → "Is this ready?" requires a Slack message.
//    → The whole team wastes time chasing content status.
//    → Custom states (In Progress, For Review, Approved) make status visible at a glance.

// 3. No editorial calendar — publishing happens when someone remembers:
//    → Content is published at random times, misaligned with campaigns.
//    → Marketing sends an email blast pointing to content that isn't live yet.
//    → Editorial calendar with PublishPress or a native scheduling discipline solves this.

// 4. DDEV used as a staging environment:
//    → Content reviewed on a local DDEV instance that differs from production.
//    → Differences in plugins, PHP version, or config cause surprises on go-live.
//    → DDEV is for local development. Staging must mirror production.

// Multilingual workflow anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. Translators start before source content is approved:
//    → Source is edited three times after translation begins.
//    → Each edit requires a retranslation round. Cost and time triples.
//    → Translation begins only after editorial approval. Lock the source first.

// 2. Each country market manages its own translations independently:
//    → "Download Lumo Pro" in English, "Lumo Pro herunterladen" in German,
//       "Obtenir Lumo Pro" in French — correct. But terminology drifts over time.
//    → Style guide with glossaries enforced in the TMS prevents drift.

// 3. No TMS — translators work directly in the WordPress backend:
//    → No translation memory: the same sentence is translated from scratch each time.
//    → No QA tools: terminology inconsistencies go unnoticed.
//    → Connect WPML to Phrase, Lokalise, or Crowdin. Translation memory alone
//       can cut translation costs by 20–40% on repeated content.

// 4. Decorative images with non-empty alt text:
//    <img src="decorative-divider.png" alt="decorative divider"> // wrong
//    <img src="decorative-divider.png" alt=""> // correct — signals "decorative" to screen readers

// Team organization anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. A bug reported by email, fixed directly on production:
//    → "Fixed it" → two days later, the same file is overwritten by another commit.
//    → The bug is back. The client's trust is gone.
//    → Every change goes through a ticket + PR + review. Never directly to production.

// 2. Communication across ten different channels:
//    → Slack DM, email, verbal, GitHub comment, Notion comment — the same bug discussed in all five.
//    → Nobody has the full picture. Decisions are repeated or forgotten.
//    → One ticket, one thread. Everything else links to the ticket.

// 3. PR merged by its author without review:
//    → A developer opens a PR for a payment flow change and merges it themselves.
//    → An obvious SQL-injection-risk pattern makes it to production unseen.
//    → Branch protection rules: require at least one approved review; restrict self-merge.

// 4. Documentation in a tool nobody opens:
//    → Architecture decisions live in a Confluence space from 2022.
//    → New developers get onboarded from memory.
//    → Markdown docs in the repo: `docs/architecture.md`, `docs/setup.md`.
//    → They are opened every time someone runs `git log`.

// Documentation and onboarding anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. Documentation lives in a tool nobody opens (Confluence from 2022):
//    → New developers get onboarded by asking the most senior team member.
//    → The senior member is unavailable → onboarding stalls.
//    → Move docs into the repo: docs/setup.md, docs/architecture.md.
//    → They are opened every time someone looks at the code.

// 2. Comments explain what the code does, not why:
//    // Add 1 to the counter
//    $counter++;
//    → Useless. The code already shows this.

//    // Increment past already-migrated posts to avoid re-running migration
//    // that was applied in WooCommerce 8.2 → 9.0 upgrade (see GitHub #1234)
//    $counter++;
//    → Valuable. This is the information a future developer cannot derive from the code.

// 3. Manual changelog — always incomplete:
//    → Developer forgets to add their fix. Client asks "was that security issue patched?"
//    → Nobody knows for certain.
//    → Automate: PR labels (fix, feature, security) + release-changelog-builder.
//       Every merged PR is automatically recorded.

// 4. One 90-minute onboarding video:
//    → One step changes in month 3. The entire video is outdated.
//    → Five short clips, each covering one topic. Update only the clip that changed.

// Knowledge retention anti-patterns from the book:

// 1. "Bus factor" of 1 on a critical module:
//    → The payment integration is understood by one developer.
//    → She is on holiday when the payment gateway returns a new error code.
//    → No documentation, no second owner. The team is stuck for three days.
//    → Ownership rotation + ADRs: two people always understand each critical module.

// 2. Architecture documented only in email threads:
//    → "Why did we use a custom table instead of post meta?"
//    → Searching through 3-year-old email threads. Or asking the original author.
//    → ADR in docs/adr/: one Markdown file, 20 lines, permanent and searchable.

// 3. Pair programming never happens because "it's twice as expensive":
//    → Developer A builds a complex REST endpoint alone.
//    → Review catches 6 issues. Fix takes 2 days. Support requests follow.
//    → Pair session (2 h): 0 review issues. 0 support requests. Net cheaper.

// 4. Ownership never rotates:
//    → The senior developer is the only person who knows the caching layer.
//    → Every cache-related incident requires waiting for her availability.
//    → Rotate ownership every 6 months. Document it in README.md.
